An evaluator can often afford to run a new model on only $k$ of the $N$ benchmarks a leaderboard holds. We ask which $k$ to run, how to infer the rest, and how large $k$ must be, on 238 embedding models and 328 MTEB tasks and on 4{,}507 open language models and 39 Open LLM Leaderboard subtasks, holding out whole organisations or families and scoring every method on benchmarks that no selection rule can choose. The floor is the error of predicting a model's scores from all its other observed ones, about 5 points on MTEB. A factor model with a greedy design criterion beats random selection by about 1.9 points of RMSE when two or three MTEB benchmarks are run and is practically equivalent to it (within a quarter of the floor) from about eight, and on the leaderboard from five of 39 (families) or eight (strongest quarter); no other MTEB inference rule we tried beats it by more than that margin. Eight MTEB benchmarks bring the error within 2 points of the floor, 50 within 1 point, and none resolves neighbouring models; the floor is 2.5 times what the model predicts, for a cause we do not find. On the leaderboard that factor is 1.1 for held-out families and 2.5 for the strongest quarter. A rule that reads $k$ from the training models alone matches the measured $k$ within one grid step when whole groups are held out, and not under a shift in strength or time.
